<!--
  The Split — Swipey's mark, as the foreground layer of the adaptive icon.

  One photograph, cut on a near-vertical diagonal and pulled apart: the grey half is the
  bin, the blue half is keep. It states the app's outcome rather than its gesture.

  ## Sizing — the part that is easy to get wrong

  The canvas is 108x108dp, but a launcher only ever shows the central 72x72dp of it, and
  it shows that region through a mask. Masks are not squares. Two circles govern:

    r = 36dp  the largest area any mask can show (a 72dp circle)
    r = 33dp  the area EVERY mask is guaranteed to show (a 66dp circle)

  An earlier cut of this file sized the mark to 64x59dp and let its corners reach r=38.9.
  On a squircle-masked launcher that looked as drawn; on a circle-masked one — which is
  what the device this was built for actually uses — the mask deleted all four corner radii
  outright, plus a slice off each side edge, and what survived was two flat slabs and a
  black bar. The rounded-rectangle silhouette is the entire reason the mark reads as a
  photograph, so clipping it does not soften the mark, it erases its meaning.

  So the shape is now contained rather than full-bleed:

    card      46 x 52dp, centred on (54,54) — x 31..77, y 28..80
    corners   r = 12dp, arc centres at (43,40) (65,40) (43,68) (65,68)
    reach     each corner's ink lands 29.8dp from centre — 3.2dp inside the 33dp circle

  Every mask shows this whole, and the ground stays visible all the way around, which is
  what gives the icon air next to its neighbours.

  ## The gap

  6dp, measured horizontally at both the top and bottom edge so the cut stays parallel
  rather than tapering; the cut leans 5dp over the card's height, because dead vertical
  reads as a battery or a progress bar. That gap is also the ONLY thing separating the two
  halves in ic_launcher_monochrome.xml once the system flattens them to one tint — do not
  narrow it.

  ## Colours

  Literals, not @color references, and deliberately so: a launcher icon must not follow the
  device's light/dark setting, and a values-night override would make it do exactly that.

  The keep half is SwipeyLightColors.keep. The bin half is icon-specific and lighter than
  SwipeyLightColors.bin (#6B7076): that token is tuned for small text on a surface, and at
  46dp a flat area of it sits as dark as the blue, so the two halves fight instead of one
  leading. Optical sizing, not a drift.

  ui/design/Mark.kt draws the same two paths in Compose for the in-app lockup, translated
  to the card's own origin. MarkGeometryTest keeps the three copies honest.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">

    <!-- Bin half. -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#9AA0A7"
        android:pathData="M43,28 H53.5 L48.5,80 H43 A12,12 0 0 1 31,68 V40 A12,12 0 0 1 43,28 Z" />

    <!-- Keep half. #1D51D6 — SwipeyLightColors.keep. -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#1D51D6"
        android:pathData="M59.5,28 H65 A12,12 0 0 1 77,40 V68 A12,12 0 0 1 65,80 H54.5 Z" />

</vector>
